{0 Logging}

Use {!Workero.Console} for one log entry at a chosen level. Use OCaml standard
output or standard error when your code already writes there. This Worker
logs a warning for a request method it does not serve.

{@ocaml[
let handler req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  match Request.method_ req with
  | GET ->
      Console.info "serving GET";
      Response.text "ok\n"
  | _ ->
      Console.warn "request method not allowed";
      Response.text ~status:`Method_Not_Allowed "method not allowed\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Run [opam exec -- wrangler dev] in one terminal and
[curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8787/] in a second terminal. The response has
status 405. Wrangler prints [request method not allowed] as a warning. Use
[opam exec -- wrangler tail] or the Cloudflare dashboard for a deployed Worker.
See {{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/observability/logs/}Workers
logs}.

{1 Console and standard output}

{!Workero.Console.log}, [info], [warn], [error], and [debug] call the global
[console]. Each call writes one entry without an OCaml buffer to flush.
Standard output and standard error also reach Workers logs, but they use
OCaml buffers. Workero flushes those buffers when a {!Workero.serve},
{!Workero.scheduled}, {!Workero.queue}, {!Workero.email}, {!Workero.tail} or
{!Workero.tail_stream} handler finishes, even if it raises. The handlers of
Durable Objects, entrypoints and Workflows also flush them.
Thus [Printf.printf] inside these handlers needs no newline or [%!] to appear
when the handler ends.

Output written after the handler ends, for example from a function given to
{!Workero.Context.wait_until}, stays in the buffer until the next handler ends.
Flush [stdout] or [stderr] there if the entry must appear at once, or use
{!Workero.Console}.
